<h2>The transfer that arrived mid-litigation</h2>
<p>The contest was the classic one: an aggrieved widow asserting her right to reside in the shared household, and the matriarchal side of the family asserting ownership. What sharpened it was timing. While the proceedings under the DV Act were pending, a gift deed was executed in respect of the property — the kind of transfer that, if given full effect, would have left the residence claim with nothing to operate upon.</p>
<p>Justice Uday Kumar declined to let the deed do that work. Applying Section 52 of the Transfer of Property Act, the Court held that a transfer effected during the pendency of a proceeding in which rights to that very property are directly and specifically in question is subject to the result of that proceeding. The gift was not erased; it was subordinated. Whatever the donee received, she received it burdened with the widow&rsquo;s claim. The Court also invoked estoppel against the family&rsquo;s attempt to resile from the position on which the widow had lived in the premises.</p>

<h2>The Supreme Court trilogy behind the result</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Satish Chander Ahuja.</strong> The shared household under Section 2(s) is not confined to property owned by the husband or the joint family of which he is a member. A house belonging to in-laws, where the woman lived in a domestic relationship, qualifies.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>S. Vanitha.</strong> Competing statutory regimes — including eviction claims by senior citizens — must be balanced against the woman&rsquo;s right of residence; neither statute automatically trumps the other.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Prabha Tyagi.</strong> The right to reside in the shared household subsists even if the aggrieved woman is not in actual occupation when she applies, and a domestic relationship once established is not defeated by the respondent&rsquo;s later devices.</div>
</div>

<h2>The accommodation charge: residence rights with a price tag</h2>
<p>The order&rsquo;s most practical feature is its alternative structure. Section 19 of the DV Act has always contained two routes: protect the woman&rsquo;s occupation of the shared household itself, or require the respondent to secure &ldquo;same level&rdquo; alternate accommodation or pay rent for it under Section 19(1)(f). The Calcutta High Court quantified the second route in advance — an interim accommodation charge of Rs 10,000 per month if equivalent accommodation is not provided. That converts a right that is often merely declared into one that is immediately enforceable: the family may choose which obligation to perform, but it cannot choose neither.</p>
<div class="note">For Delhi practice, the same architecture applies. Residence orders from the Mahila Courts routinely pair occupation protection with an alternative accommodation or rent direction, and a prayer under Section 19(1)(f) should be pleaded in the alternative in every residence application.</div>

<h2>Drafting lessons for both sides</h2>
<p>For the aggrieved person, two averments matter most: the facts establishing the property as a shared household within Section 2(s) — residence in a domestic relationship, not ownership — and a specific challenge, with a lis pendens plea, to any transfer executed after proceedings began. Where a transfer is apprehended rather than completed, an application under Section 19(1)(d) and (e) seeking restraint on alienation should be moved at the first hearing rather than after the deed is registered.</p>
<p>For respondents and transferees, the decision is a caution against treating registration as a cure. A gift or sale executed mid-proceedings does not confer possession-proof title; it acquires the litigation along with the property. The honest alternatives are to contest the shared-household characterisation on its facts, or to offer compliant alternate accommodation under Section 19(1)(f) — the one route the statute itself respects.</p>
